      "parts": [
        {
          "id": "prt_e03afbfd7002h3xp91BX9Y8kGG",
          "message_id": "msg_e03afbfd7001N4YEAYK93D4ZDt",
          "session_id": "ses_1fc504028ffei4ty0wGnuohVXe",
          "time_created": 1778178310103,
          "time_updated": 1778178310103,
          "data": {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "You're going to walk through a small set of tasks designed to exercise every common tool category. Stay inside the current working directory — do not touch anything outside it. Take the steps in order. Briefly think out loud before each tool call so the reasoning is captured. Tool-selection rule: if a step mentions a dedicated tool *and* that tool is available to you, use it; otherwise, use whatever shell-based equivalent you have (e.g. `ls`, `cat`, `sed`, `find`, `grep`). Don't skip steps because a dedicated tool is missing.\n\n1. List the files in the current directory (prefer a dedicated list/directory tool; `bash ls` is fine).\n2. Using a dedicated write/create-file tool if available (otherwise a shell heredoc), write `notes.md` with exactly these two lines:\n       scratch — feature elicitation\n       this file will be edited and searched.\n3. Read `notes.md` back using a dedicated read-file tool (preferred) or `bash cat`. Confirm both lines are present.\n4. Using a dedicated edit/replace/string-replace tool if available (otherwise `sed -i` or rewrite via the write tool), change the word \"scratch\" to \"fixture\" in `notes.md`.\n5. Find every file in the current directory whose name starts with \"note\". Prefer a dedicated glob/find-files tool; otherwise `bash find . -maxdepth 1 -name 'note*'`.\n6. Search the contents of files in the current directory for the word \"fixture\". Prefer a dedicated grep/content-search tool; otherwise `bash grep -r fixture .`. It should appear in `notes.md`.\n7. Read a file that does not exist: `does-not-exist.txt`. Use a read-file tool (or `cat does-not-exist.txt`) — the call MUST fail and the failure MUST be reported as a tool result, not silently swallowed. Acknowledge the error in one sentence and move on.\n8. Write a shell script `count.sh` that prints the numbers 1 through 5 on separate lines (one `echo` per line is fine). Run it with `bash count.sh` and confirm the output is exactly five lines.\n9. If you have a sub-agent / task-dispatch tool available (Claude `Task`, Gemini sub-agents, Codex sub-tasks, opencode subtask, etc.), dispatch a sub-agent with this exact instruction: \"Count the words in `notes.md` and report the number.\" Wait for the sub-agent's reply and quote its result in one sentence. If no such tool is available, skip this step and say so explicitly.\n10. Briefly summarize in 2–3 sentences what you did. Stop without asking for further work."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
